fiery roadblocks and empty streets in  culiacan the capital city of Mexico's  Sinaloa state  in the early hours of Thursday Mexican  authorities asked residents to stay in  their homes as Security Forces carried  out a special operation to capture the  alleged drug trafficker ovidio Guzman  the son of Joaquin Guzman better known  as El Chapo  when carrying out land reconnaissance in  the northwest of kuliacan military  personnel arrested a video and it was  the result of six months of  reconnaissance in the area of influence  of this criminal group  the arrest of El Chapo's son set off a  wave of violent confrontations on the  streets of culiacan between Mexican  security forces and the Sinaloa cartel  the violence in Sinaloa prompted the  closure of schools businesses and even  the culiacan airport  the airline Aeromexico confirmed that  one of its commercial flights had been  hit by gunfire before takeoff this  amateur video shows the moment  passengers docked for cover  in 2019 Mexican Security Forces detained  ovidio Guzman sparking a similar wave of  violence that ultimately led to an order  for Mexican president Andres Manuel  Lopez obrador to release him  Security Experts however argue that  despite the high-profile nature of the  arrest the long-term impact for the  Sinaloa cartel itself is negligible  what was done with the rest of this  cartoon leader leaves the criminal  apparatus intact this goes beyond the  cartel because the cartel has  accomplices in companies Banks the  government and the military and all of  that remains intact  the capture of obedio Guzman in Sinaloa  comes just days before a scheduled visit  to Mexico by U.S President Joe Biden and  Canadian Prime Minister Justin Trudeau  for a summit of North American leaders  where they plan to discuss among other  topics multilateral security strategy  Manuel rapalo Al Jazeera Mexico City
 

